## Verdict: REQUEST CHANGES

Fetched and reviewed PR #34 at the requested head:

```text
PR_HEAD=de2a9c32641b9d40c4254301e2e01928136a15fe
MAIN_HEAD=7b0502827ad7d84c5c8b312221c5bf9754709642
```

The first-line anchoring fixes the previous whole-body match, but it still recognizes a prefix rather than the complete attribution syntax. More importantly, the fetched `de2a9c3` tree contains no tree-versus-PR-head guard: a known PR head that is not an ancestor of the working tree still launches Codex and derives a verdict.

### Finding 1 — first-line anchoring still accepts prose that starts with the marker

**Severity: minor**

The filter applies `startswith("**sana**")`, but `progress` emits the more specific prefix `**sana** · `. A native or manually written comment whose first line begins with a bold mention—such as `**sana** please review this note`—is still attributed to Sana.

This narrows the original bug again rather than removing the ambiguity. Match the complete producer-owned attribution prefix, including its delimiter, instead of only the bold token.

✅ Executed against `de2a9c3`:

```bash
python3 - <<'PY'
import importlib.util, io, contextlib
p='.review-pr34/q-system/.q-system/scripts/linear-sync.py'
s=importlib.util.spec_from_file_location('linear_sync',p)
m=importlib.util.module_from_spec(s)
s.loader.exec_module(m)

class A:
    issue='ASK-221'
    agent='sana'
    last=0

m.graphql=lambda *_: {
    'issue': {
        'identifier':'ASK-221',
        'title':'first-line prose',
        'comments': {
            'nodes': [
                {
                    'createdAt':'2026-07-30T00:00:00Z',
                    'body':'**sana** please review this note\n\n'
                           'Not emitted by progress and not authored by Sana.',
                    'user':{'name':'Codex'},
                    'botActor':None,
                },
                {
                    'createdAt':'2026-07-30T00:01:00Z',
                    'body':'**sana** · 2026-07-30 00:01 UTC\n\n'
                           'Actual progress comment.',
                    'user':{'name':'Assaf'},
                    'botActor':None,
                },
            ]
        },
    }
}

out=io.StringIO()
with contextlib.redirect_stdout(out):
    rc=m.cmd_comments(A())
print('rc=', rc, sep='')
print(out.getvalue(), end='')
PY
```

Real output:

```text
rc=0
ASK-221: first-line prose  (2 comment(s))

--- 2026-07-30T00:00:00Z · via Codex ---
**sana** please review this note

Not emitted by progress and not authored by Sana.

--- 2026-07-30T00:01:00Z · via Assaf ---
**sana** · 2026-07-30 00:01 UTC

Actual progress comment.
```

The output should contain only the second comment.

### Finding 2 — the promised tree-versus-PR-head guard is absent

**Severity: major**

After reading `headRefOid`, `pr-review-agent.sh` immediately computes the review round and dispatches the engine. The fetched target has no `git cat-file`, `git merge-base --is-ancestor`, or equivalent check between SHA capture and reviewer execution.

Consequently, even when the reported PR head is a known commit and is not an ancestor of the working tree’s `HEAD`, Codex runs and the script exits successfully. With `--post`, that verdict can be attached to the reported PR SHA despite having been produced from a different local tree. The unattended gate can therefore publish an authoritative verdict whose file references do not describe the reviewed PR tree.

The reproducer created a known commit object one commit ahead of `de2a9c3`, returned it as `headRefOid`, left the working tree at `de2a9c3`, and used a stub Codex executable as the observable dispatch sink.

✅ Executed against `de2a9c3`:

```bash
TREE=$(git rev-parse refs/codex-review/pr34^{tree})
FAKE_HEAD=$(printf 'known PR head not in working tree\n' |
  git commit-tree "$TREE" -p refs/codex-review/pr34)

# Stub gh returns $FAKE_HEAD as headRefOid.
# Stub codex records whether it was invoked.
PATH="$D/bin:$PATH" HOME="$D/home" KIPI_REVIEW_CODEX_MODEL=test-model \
  bash .review-pr34/q-system/.q-system/scripts/pr-review-agent.sh \
  34 --engine codex
```

Real output:

```text
worktree_head=de2a9c32641b9d40c4254301e2e01928136a15fe
reported_pr_head=4c747508b6b584028b0c88e7d39768df0e80a961
object_known=yes
reported_head_is_ancestor=no
reviewer_rc=0
codex_ran=yes
2026-07-30T00:29:55Z reviewing PR #34: wrong-tree guard repro
  head sha under review: 4c747508b6b584028b0c88e7d39768df0e80a961
  round: 1 (engine: codex)
2026-07-30T00:29:55Z running the codex reviewer (bounded at 2400s)...
2026-07-30T00:29:55Z review written: .../pr-34-20260730-002955.md
  verdict: APPROVE
2026-07-30T00:29:56Z done
```

Expected behavior was refusal before `codex_ran=yes`.

## What is sound

* **The requested commit was fetched and verified.** The full SHA resolved to `de2a9c32641b9d40c4254301e2e01928136a15fe`.
* **The previous whole-body-marker defect is fixed.** The filter now inspects only the first line, so a `**sana**` marker appearing later in another author’s prose no longer matches.
* **The regression suite exercises the prior reproducer.**  
  ✅ `python3 .review-pr34/q-system/.q-system/scripts/test_linear_sync_comments.py` produced `13 passed, 0 failed`.
* **A similarly named attributed agent does not collide.** I tried `**sana-ops** · …` while filtering for `sana`; it did not match because the closing Markdown delimiter prevents that prefix collision.
* **The fetched tree was executed without modifying it.** The temporary worktree and review refs were removed afterward.
* **The original checkout remains clean.**  
  ✅ `git status --short --branch` produced `## work`.
* **The excluded multi-block parser hazard was not re-filed or reassessed.**

